In the Stratos Datacenter, our application server 1 is encountering performance degradation due to excessive processes held by the nfsuser user. 
To mitigate this issue, we need to enforce limitations on its maximum processes. 
Please set the maximum process limits as specified below:

a. Set the soft limit to 1025

b. Set the hard limit to 2027

SOLUTION
To enforce the process limits for nfsuser on Application Server 1, you need to edit the PAM limits configuration file. The values you specified (soft: 1025, hard: 2027) should be added directly to /etc/security/limits.conf.

⚙️ Configuration Steps
Log in to the server (Application Server 1 in Stratos Datacenter) and switch to the root user.

Edit the limits file using a text editor like vi or nano:

bash
sudo vi /etc/security/limits.conf
Add the following lines at the end of the file. This uses the nproc item to limit the number of processes :

text
nfsuser soft nproc 1025
nfsuser hard nproc 2027
soft: The limit that is enforced by default. The user can temporarily exceed this up to the hard limit .

hard: The absolute maximum limit. The user cannot exceed this value .

Save and exit the file.

✅ Verification
The changes will take effect the next time nfsuser starts a new session (logs in). You can verify the configuration by switching to the user and checking the limits:

bash
# Check the soft limit
su - nfsuser -c "ulimit -u"

# Check the hard limit
su - nfsuser -c "ulimit -u -H"
The first command should output 1025, and the second should output 2027.

Note: Depending on your specific Linux distribution (e.g., RHEL/CentOS), there might be a file in /etc/security/limits.d/ (like 90-nproc.conf) that could override these settings. If the limits do not apply after logging in, check that directory for conflicting entries .


